<h1>AI Internal Linking</h1>
<p class="sub">What the plugin does, how it decides, and what it will never do. Version 0.15.0.</p>

<h2>In one paragraph</h2>

<p>The plugin reads your whole site, works out which pages should link to which, and proposes each link with the exact wording to use and the sentence it would sit in. You approve or reject each one. Approved links are written into the content with two independent ways to undo them. It runs on any WordPress site, needs no configuration and no API key, and has an optional AI layer on top if you want one.</p>

<h2>How a link gets suggested</h2>

<p>Three engines run in order on every page. Each draws from the same link budget, so the strongest evidence wins.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Order</th><th>Engine</th><th>The evidence it uses</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>GSC keyword</td><td>Search Console: a query the target page already ranks for, said on this page without a link</td><td>Free</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>AI Suggestion</td><td>A chat model reading the page and a shortlist of genuinely related pages</td><td>Your own API key</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Related Content</td><td>Vocabulary overlap, computed locally from your own content</td><td>Free</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Search demand is stronger evidence than similarity, which is why the keyword engine runs first. The free engine runs last and fills whatever budget is left, so you always get suggestions even with no key configured.</p>

<div class="key"><strong>The rule behind all three:</strong> the plugin links words that are already on your page. It never writes new sentences, never invents an anchor, and never inserts anything you have not approved.</div>

<h2>What it will never do</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Never</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Insert a link without approval</td><td>Every suggestion waits in a review inbox. Nothing reaches your content until you click.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Invent anchor text</td><td>The anchor must already appear, word for word, in the page. If it does not, no suggestion is made at all.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link inside a heading</td><td>Headings and the page title are stripped before the plugin reads anything, so they cannot be linked.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link across languages</td><td>An English page is never linked to a Hindi one, on any multilingual setup.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rewrite builder content</td><td>Elementor, Divi, WPBakery, Beaver Builder and ACF pages are suggest-only. The plugin never touches content a builder owns.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Over-link a page</td><td>A density ceiling per 1,000 words, a per-page cap, and a limit on repeating the same exact anchor to one target.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Send your content anywhere without a key</td><td>With no API key configured, the plugin makes no external calls at all.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Applying a link, and undoing it</h2>

<p>Approving a link does write to your post. That is the point of the feature, so the safety around it is worth stating precisely.</p>

<p>Before a single character changes, the plugin saves a WordPress revision AND writes the original content to its own backup ledger. Two independent ways back, because a revision can be pruned by another plugin and the ledger cannot.</p>

<p>The insertion itself is a byte-preserving splice: the plugin finds the exact phrase and wraps it, rather than parsing your HTML and writing it back out. That distinction matters, because rebuilding HTML from a parser quietly corrupts real world markup. Every write is checked before saving, to confirm the visible text is unchanged.</p>

<p>Inserted links are plain <code>&lt;a data-ailinking-id&gt;</code> tags, never shortcodes. If the plugin were deleted from the filesystem tomorrow, what remains in your content is an ordinary working hyperlink, not broken markup. Uninstalling properly restores the original content from the ledger first.</p>

<div class="note"><strong>Undo comes in two sizes.</strong> One click reverses a single link. A batched action reverses every link the plugin has ever inserted, in one go. If a post was edited after a link was applied, undo warns you before overwriting those edits.</div>

<h2>What you see in the admin</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>Tab</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Setup &amp; Dashboard</td><td>Which content to include, indexing, running a scan with pause, resume and stop, and how much of each page the AI reads</td></tr>
<tr><td>Suggestions</td><td>The review inbox: approve, reject, apply, undo, filtered by status and by which engine proposed it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Link Health</td><td>Orphans, dead ends, broken links, over and under-linked pages, internal PageRank, anchor diversity, click depth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Connect GSC</td><td>Google Search Console, connected with a service account. Generic keyword CSV import as a fallback</td></tr>
<tr><td>AI Keys</td><td>Your API keys, encrypted at rest, with token usage and estimated cost per key and per model</td></tr>
<tr><td>Settings</td><td>Engine toggles, link density, provider choice, monthly spend cap</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>If you use an API key</h2>

<p>Keys are yours. There is no vendor account, no proxy and no middleman: the plugin talks directly to whichever provider you configured, using your key. Keys are encrypted before storage, are never shown in full, are never written to logs, and are never sent to the browser.</p>

<h3>Knowing what it costs, before it costs it</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Control</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
<tr><td>Live ticker</td><td>Tokens and estimated cost accumulating while a scan runs, so you watch the spend rather than discover it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Usage breakdown</td><td>This month and all time, per key and per model, on the AI Keys tab</td></tr>
<tr><td>Monthly spend cap</td><td>Stops AI calls before the cap is exceeded. The free engines keep working</td></tr>
<tr><td>Words per page</td><td>How much of each page the AI reads. The main cost lever, since every post in a scan is one request</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="note"><strong>The AI reads part of a page, not all of it.</strong> By default the first 1,000 words, so on a long article the AI engine proposes links from the opening section only. Raise "Words per page sent to the AI" on Setup and Dashboard to widen that: presets go to 3,000 words, and a custom value up to 20,000. This applies to the AI engine alone. The two free engines always read the entire page.</div>

<h2>Requirements</h2>

<table>
<tr><th>WordPress</th><td>6.2 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><th>PHP</th><td>7.4 or newer</td></tr>
<tr><th>API key</th><td>Optional. Everything except the AI engine works without one</td></tr>
<tr><th>Search Console</th><td>Optional. Improves suggestion quality when connected</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="footer">
AI Internal Linking, version 0.15.0. For the scoring formulas behind every decision, see the companion document "AI Internal Linking, Scoring Formulas".<br />
